{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨y,\\,x-z,\\,-2x⟩\\cdot ⟨2x,\\,-z,\\,y⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy-z(x-z)-2xy}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{z}^{2}-xz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{{z}^{2}-xz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-161210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2xy+{z}^{2}-xz"] ["math" "2xy-{z}^{2}"] ["math" "xy+2{z}^{2}-xz"] ["math" "{z}^{2}-xyz"] ["math" "{z}^{2}-xz"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{z}^{2}-xz"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-160838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-160838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(2x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-160838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-160838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-160838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2xy+yz+xz"] ["math" "2xy-yz+2xz"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}+2yz"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}+3yz"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}+yz"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-160838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{x}^{2}+yz"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(xy-yz+2xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}xy-yz+2xz\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-161200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}"] ["math" "3xz-yz"] ["math" "xy+yz+xz"] ["math" "xy-2yz+xz"] ["math" "xy-yz+2xz"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "xy-yz+2xz"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{z}^{2}-x+2(y-{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y-x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2y-x}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}2y-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-161198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2y+{z}^{2}"] ["math" "2y-x"] ["math" "2y-x-2{z}^{2}"] ["math" "x+2{z}^{2}"] ["math" "x+y+{z}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2y-x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3(1-{e}^{z})+xy-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}xy-3{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-161199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{e}^{z}+xy+3"] ["math" "xy+6"] ["math" "xy+{e}^{z}"] ["math" "xy-3-{e}^{z}"] ["math" "xy-3{e}^{z}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "xy-3{e}^{z}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,2,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-365878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,x,\\,3x+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=-1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 2,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,x,\\,3x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(3x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(-(3x+y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{2}[(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+xy){|}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[6y+{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-365882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for  $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,4x,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-161192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,y,\\,4x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}4x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot {[2{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-365879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+2y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=2$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(x+2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-(x+2y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2xy){|}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{y}{2}+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-365880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-365880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x-y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{6}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,6-y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+3)+(x+y)+(6-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-162154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x+2y+1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+1,\\,-y,\\,4x+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-x-2y-1+2y+4x+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(3x+2)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162540" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-162540" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162540" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162540" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{11}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=3x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨y,\\,x,\\,3x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3y-x-(3x-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}}(4y-4x)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4y-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[4xy-2{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4y-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{2}-2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162546" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-162546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162546" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2y-x-5$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 0.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{6}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2y-x-5$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-5,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=5.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2y-x-5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2y,\\,\\frac{x}{2},\\,2y-x-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2y-x+2y-x-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(-2x-5)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}}(2x+5)\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[y]_{-1}^{0}\\cdot [{x}^{2}+5x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(0+1)\\cdot (1+5-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-161194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-161194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1,\\,y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-x+(x-2y-2)+2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{3}})\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d31cc7f2ff5ad38c5338074267c26ac0876f83ad0a3d14e4c7d79fb12b40bff"} {"question" "q-162147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "q-162147" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a0d9e7462959536682cf01e908dc7155f8a54cc9bb91ae7f5d08b48575335b3"} {"question" "e-12356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/assets/35bcfa2242051bed034ac3343c1a6d67f94916d4267030f726e2bd27cbbe02f1.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/example-12356.json" "file_sha256" "5e85799a4463330acb574d2b0cd5803e5d933f8e5e1e03324954f7c3febec4ac"} {"question" "e-12356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{(z)^{2}+(-y)^{2}+(-x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz-2yz-x(z-y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/example-12356.json" "file_sha256" "5e85799a4463330acb574d2b0cd5803e5d933f8e5e1e03324954f7c3febec4ac"} {"question" "e-12384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/example-12384.json" "file_sha256" "646f06963eb907efc75de727eed9b17da39c36325b1d801405d46e88dc649775"} {"question" "e-12384" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,z,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,4xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/example-12384.json" "file_sha256" "646f06963eb907efc75de727eed9b17da39c36325b1d801405d46e88dc649775"} {"question" "e-12454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1.$ Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||\\mathbf r_x \\times \\mathbf r_y|| =3.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/example-12454.json" "file_sha256" "35f225c103530af62bfd614fa909acf5965cfa8fe319932fde408b1a036de7c3"} {"question" "e-12454" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,0,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2y+0x+2x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}x\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}x\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[y]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{x}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067034/example-12454.json" "file_sha256" "35f225c103530af62bfd614fa909acf5965cfa8fe319932fde408b1a036de7c3"}] "usage" {"e-12384" [] "q-160838" [] "e-12356" [] "e-12454" []} "basis" 2222 "retractions" [[17592186167064 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $n^(~)$, where\n\n$F(x, y, z) = ⟨z, y, x⟩, n^(~)(x, y, z) = ⟨2y,-z, 2x⟩, n = (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖)$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(S)\\frac{AAAAAAA}{\\sqrt{4x^{2} + 4y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186166417 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$, where\n\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!, \\quad \\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}$.\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186166417 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}$. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨z,-y,-x⟩)/(\\sqrt{(z)^{2} + (-y)^{2} + (-x)^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{z^{2} + y^{2} + x^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot \\frac{⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{⟨x, 2z, z - y⟩ \\cdot ⟨z,-y,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (xz - 2yz - x(z - y))/(\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{xy - 2yz}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{x y - 2 y z}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}dS\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166418 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle z,\\: z,\\: 4xy\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 1$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$."] [17592186166418 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x\\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0\\leq y\\leq 1\n$$\n\nthe downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨z, z, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1, 4xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩ \\\\\n&=-4xy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)(-4xy)dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-4xy)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}(-4xy)dxdy \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{1}2ydy∫_{0}^{1}2xdx \\\\\n&= - [y^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166419 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle y,\\: 0,\\: z\\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y$ for $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 1$. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$.\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r_x' \\times \\mathbf r_y' \\mid \\mid =3$.*"] [17592186166419 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface\n\n$$\nz = 2x+2y\n$$\n\ncan be written using the dot product as\n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = 0, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 0\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S$, we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (n^(~))/(‖n^(~)‖) \\\\\n&= (⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩)/(\\sqrt{(-2)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 1^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nz=2x+2y\n$$\n\non $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨y, 0, z⟩ \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}⟨y, 0, 2x + 2y⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2,-2, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-2y + 0x + 2x + 2y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(2x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)\\frac{2}{3}xdS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot \\mid \\mid r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} \\mid \\mid dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)\\frac{2}{3}x \\cdot 3dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2xdA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}2xdxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}dy \\cdot ∫_{0}^{2}2xdx \\\\\n&= [y]_{0}^{1} \\cdot [x^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163803 17592186163804 17592186163805 17592186163806 17592186163807] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163802 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163802 :answer-field/id] [17592186163802 :answer-field/key] [17592186163802 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
